1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,455
By the winter of twenty forty-six, the President of
the United States had two problems,

2
00:00:13,475 --> 00:00:15,477
and they were the same problem.

3
00:00:16,047 --> 00:00:21,863
The first was that forty-one million Americans took a
medicine that only one company made,

4
00:00:21,883 --> 00:00:27,633
and that ninety-one percent of them, in every poll
her office ran, said they trusted that

5
00:00:27,653 --> 00:00:33,469
company more than they trusted their government. The
second was that she had been elected,

6
00:00:33,489 --> 00:00:37,488
eighteen months before, on a promise to
do something about it.

7
00:00:38,058 --> 00:00:43,689
She was sixty-three. She had been a governor, and a
prosecutor before that, and she was

8
00:00:43,709 --> 00:00:49,275
nobody's fool, and she had spent her first year
discovering, department by department,

9
00:00:49,295 --> 00:00:54,730
exactly how deep the roots had gone while everyone
was arguing about something else.

10
00:00:56,801 --> 00:01:03,073
In January, her national security adviser had given
her a document that four people had

11
00:01:03,093 --> 00:01:09,509
read. It was an assessment of what would happen if
the United States government attempted

12
00:01:09,529 --> 00:01:14,716
to seize Umbrella Life Sciences' domestic
assets under emergency powers.

13
00:01:15,286 --> 00:01:21,210
The conclusion was on the first page, in one
sentence: the United States does not possess

14
00:01:21,230 --> 00:01:27,221
the capacity to do this without causing the deaths of
a number of its own citizens greater

15
00:01:27,241 --> 00:01:30,761
than any war it has fought since
eighteen sixty-five.

16
00:01:33,331 --> 00:01:37,356
Owen Carrick was fifty-eight when he
finally got into the building.

17
00:01:37,927 --> 00:01:44,130
It had taken him sixteen years. He had shown the
bullets to four journalists, two members

18
00:01:44,150 --> 00:01:50,354
of Congress, a Pentagon official, and a priest, and
then to a Norwegian heiress, and then

19
00:01:50,374 --> 00:01:56,577
to a task force that had been dismantled around him.
And in February of twenty forty-six,

20
00:01:56,597 --> 00:02:02,661
a deputy national security adviser who had read
every Lantern report and had a daughter

21
00:02:02,681 --> 00:02:08,885
on Aeterna asked him to come in on a Tuesday morning
and tell the President of the United

22
00:02:08,905 --> 00:02:11,123
States what he thought was true.

23
00:02:11,693 --> 00:02:17,399
He had forty minutes. He had prepared for eleven
days. He did not bring the jam jar; they

24
00:02:17,419 --> 00:02:20,165
would not let him through the
gate with it.

25
00:02:20,855 --> 00:02:26,053
Ma'am, I'm going to say four things that sound
insane, and then I'm going to give you

26
00:02:26,073 --> 00:02:29,551
the evidence for each one, and then
you can throw me out.

27
00:02:30,072 --> 00:02:35,810
He said them. He was very clear, and very calm, and
he had rehearsed it so many times

28
00:02:35,830 --> 00:02:37,775
that he did not stumble once.

29
00:02:38,344 --> 00:02:44,606
One: Albert Wesker was not a human being, in the
ordinary sense. Two: he had been in possession

30
00:02:44,626 --> 00:02:50,953
of capabilities that no institution on Earth could
account for since at least twenty twenty-six.

31
00:02:50,973 --> 00:02:56,771
Three: everything Umbrella had built — the medicines,
the Foundation, the Territory, the

32
00:02:56,791 --> 00:03:02,589
frameworks of association — was not the goal, but the
method. And four: he intended, and

33
00:03:02,609 --> 00:03:04,507
had always intended, to rule.

34
00:03:06,697 --> 00:03:09,909
Mister Carrick. Do you know what the worst
part of this briefing is?

35
00:03:10,430 --> 00:03:11,144
Ma'am?

36
00:03:11,663 --> 00:03:13,286
That I already believe you.

37
00:03:15,686 --> 00:03:20,474
I've had this conversation four times in fourteen
months, with four different people,

38
00:03:20,494 --> 00:03:25,623
each of whom thought they were the first. The Bureau
believes you. Half of Langley believes

39
00:03:25,643 --> 00:03:30,658
you. My science adviser believes you and can't say
so, because the moment she does, she's

40
00:03:30,678 --> 00:03:31,110
a crank.

41
00:03:31,512 --> 00:03:32,750
And it doesn't matter.

42
00:03:33,270 --> 00:03:34,254
Why not?

43
00:03:34,776 --> 00:03:39,508
Because I asked the Joint Chiefs to give me options
in January, and they gave me a page

44
00:03:39,528 --> 00:03:44,314
that says a hundred and twenty thousand Americans die
in the first eleven weeks. Because

45
00:03:44,334 --> 00:03:49,012
forty-one million people would rather have their
medicine than their sovereignty and I

46
00:03:49,032 --> 00:03:53,764
cannot say that out loud. Because he has never
broken a law in this country that we can

47
00:03:53,784 --> 00:03:58,571
prove, and because he owns the only factory on Earth
that makes eight things my citizens

48
00:03:58,591 --> 00:03:59,609
need to stay alive.

49
00:04:01,508 --> 00:04:06,321
You caught him, Mister Carrick. Sixteen years ago,
on a dashcam, in a parking lot. You

50
00:04:06,341 --> 00:04:11,435
were right about all of it, and nobody believed you,
and by the time we did, it was already

51
00:04:11,455 --> 00:04:12,502
too late to matter.

52
00:04:15,022 --> 00:04:21,019
Owen Carrick sat in the Oval Office and understood,
at last, the thing that he had spent

53
00:04:21,038 --> 00:04:24,026
his whole adult life refusing
to understand.

54
00:04:24,596 --> 00:04:30,108
He had always believed that the problem was that
nobody knew. That if he could only get

55
00:04:30,128 --> 00:04:35,513
the evidence in front of the right person, the
machinery of the world would turn, and

56
00:04:35,533 --> 00:04:37,038
something would be done.

57
00:04:37,608 --> 00:04:42,089
The machinery knew. It had known for years.
It simply could not move.

58
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:50,883
The Director came to Washington eleven days later,
at the President's invitation, and

59
00:04:50,903 --> 00:04:51,829
he came alone.

60
00:04:52,399 --> 00:04:57,843
That was a decision, and it was the whole meeting.
He had a security force of nineteen

61
00:04:57,863 --> 00:05:03,561
thousand, a private air fleet, and a personal risk
profile that four intelligence services

62
00:05:03,581 --> 00:05:09,279
had assessed as the highest of any individual alive.
He arrived in one car, with a driver,

63
00:05:09,299 --> 00:05:15,061
and walked through the North Portico with his hands in
his coat pockets, and let the Secret

64
00:05:15,081 --> 00:05:18,491
Service search him, and stood there
while they did it.

65
00:05:19,062 --> 00:05:25,284
They met alone. No aides, no notetaker, no recording
— which was itself unprecedented,

66
00:05:25,304 --> 00:05:30,148
and which her chief of staff argued
against for two days, and lost.

67
00:05:30,838 --> 00:05:35,910
I'll be direct, Doctor Wesker. I've been advised
that my government cannot compel you

68
00:05:35,930 --> 00:05:41,061
to do anything. I've also been advised that any
attempt to try would kill a great many

69
00:05:41,081 --> 00:05:44,237
of my citizens. I don't enjoy being
in that position.

70
00:05:44,757 --> 00:05:45,986
No. I wouldn't either.

71
00:05:46,505 --> 00:05:51,286
So I'm going to ask you a question, and I'd like an
honest answer, and I have nothing

72
00:05:51,305 --> 00:05:53,206
to trade for it. What do you want?

73
00:05:55,725 --> 00:06:01,443
He took off his sunglasses. He did that, in this
world, perhaps four times a year, and

74
00:06:01,463 --> 00:06:03,845
every single time it was a decision.

75
00:06:04,535 --> 00:06:08,590
I want the same thing you do, Madam
President. I want it to work.

76
00:06:09,110 --> 00:06:10,367
Want what to work?

77
00:06:10,887 --> 00:06:16,259
All of it. The species. You've been in office for
eighteen months. You've seen the actual

78
00:06:16,279 --> 00:06:21,470
numbers — the water tables, the heat, the debt, the
demographics, the four wars nobody

79
00:06:21,490 --> 00:06:26,923
can stop. You know what your grandchildren's century
looks like on the current trajectory.

80
00:06:26,943 --> 00:06:32,073
Everyone in this building knows. And every one of
you has worked out, privately, that

81
00:06:32,093 --> 00:06:37,648
your system cannot fix it, because your system is
designed to produce a new set of temporary

82
00:06:37,668 --> 00:06:42,859
people every four years, each of whom is punished
for doing anything that takes longer

83
00:06:42,879 --> 00:06:43,465
than that.

84
00:06:44,865 --> 00:06:49,429
I am not a temporary person. That is the
entire difference between us.

85
00:06:51,449 --> 00:06:52,944
And what does that make you?

86
00:06:53,463 --> 00:06:54,232
Patient.

87
00:06:56,752 --> 00:07:02,074
She did not say anything for a while. The fire
made the only noise in the room.

88
00:07:02,764 --> 00:07:07,584
My mother is eighty-nine. She's been on Aeterna
for nine years. She gardens.

89
00:07:08,105 --> 00:07:08,789
I know.

90
00:07:09,309 --> 00:07:10,548
Of course you do.

91
00:07:11,947 --> 00:07:16,512
When you take this country — and I've read enough to
know that you intend to, and that

92
00:07:16,532 --> 00:07:21,097
you'll do it with frameworks and water systems and
not with soldiers — how long do you

93
00:07:21,117 --> 00:07:21,896
think it takes?

94
00:07:23,916 --> 00:07:29,377
If I am careless? Forty years. If I am not? You won't be
able to point at the day it happened.

95
00:07:31,899 --> 00:07:35,331
That's the most honest thing anyone has
said to me in this office.

96
00:07:35,851 --> 00:07:41,301
It's the most honest thing anyone will say to you
in this office. That's not a boast.

97
00:07:41,321 --> 00:07:43,038
It's the nature of the job.

98
00:07:46,059 --> 00:07:50,318
Owen Carrick went home and did not get out
of the chair for two days.

99
00:07:50,889 --> 00:07:56,650
He was fifty-eight. He had been an FBI agent for
seventeen years and a man who knew for

100
00:07:56,670 --> 00:08:02,830
sixteen. He had lost his badge, his pension, his
marriage, and eleven years of two daughters'

101
00:08:02,850 --> 00:08:08,744
childhoods, which was the loss that actually mattered
and which he had never once allowed

102
00:08:08,764 --> 00:08:09,941
himself to add up.

103
00:08:10,511 --> 00:08:16,800
Laura had remarried. She had told him about it
kindly, on the phone, in twenty forty-one.

104
00:08:16,820 --> 00:08:22,967
His elder daughter was a doctor in Portland. She had
taken Aeterna, as a precaution, at

105
00:08:22,987 --> 00:08:26,512
twenty-nine, because everyone in
her hospital did.

106
00:08:28,581 --> 00:08:31,165
He looked at the jam jar for a long time.

107
00:08:31,735 --> 00:08:38,508
And he found — this is the part he never told anyone,
not Haddad, not Halvorsen, not Hannah

108
00:08:38,528 --> 00:08:41,644
Price — that what he felt was not despair.

109
00:08:42,214 --> 00:08:43,409
It was relief.

110
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:49,594
For sixteen years he had believed that he was the
only thing standing between the world

111
00:08:49,614 --> 00:08:55,228
and Albert Wesker. It had been the worst kind of
loneliness, and the most complete kind

112
00:08:55,248 --> 00:09:00,732
of purpose, and it had eaten his entire life. And
in an office in Washington, a woman

113
00:09:00,752 --> 00:09:06,237
with a nuclear arsenal had told him: we know, we
all know, and there is nothing to be

114
00:09:06,257 --> 00:09:06,560
done.

115
00:09:07,129 --> 00:09:12,979
He was not the only thing standing anymore. He was
not standing at all. Nobody was.

116
00:09:15,548 --> 00:09:21,048
On the third morning he got up, and showered, and
packed a bag, and drove to Portland,

117
00:09:21,068 --> 00:09:26,632
Oregon, and knocked on his daughter's door on a
Saturday morning without calling ahead.

118
00:09:27,201 --> 00:09:32,688
She let him in. It took eleven months before she
stopped being angry with him and four

119
00:09:32,708 --> 00:09:38,259
years before they could talk about anything that
mattered, and he had both. Aeterna had

120
00:09:38,279 --> 00:09:44,150
given him thirty more years, and he had started taking
it in twenty forty-one, like everyone

121
00:09:44,170 --> 00:09:46,200
else, because everyone else did.

122
00:09:46,769 --> 00:09:53,148
That is how Owen Carrick's war ended. Not in a
courtroom, or a desert, or a corridor under

123
00:09:53,168 --> 00:09:59,334
a brick building. In a kitchen in Portland, with a
daughter who did not want to talk to

124
00:09:59,354 --> 00:10:02,960
him, and a jam jar left on a shelf
in Pennsylvania.

125
00:10:05,528 --> 00:10:11,110
He lived a long time. He saw all of it. He saw the
Accord, and the ships, and the things

126
00:10:11,130 --> 00:10:16,775
that came back from the dark with Umbrella's mark on
them, and he told his grandchildren,

127
00:10:16,795 --> 00:10:22,440
when they were old enough, that he had once shot a
man fifteen times and been handed back

128
00:10:22,460 --> 00:10:23,204
the bullets.

129
00:10:23,774 --> 00:10:26,144
They thought it was a story. He let them.

130
00:10:29,715 --> 00:10:34,563
On the flight back to Arklay, the Director sat
in the dark and did not work.

131
00:10:35,253 --> 00:10:36,833
She capitulated.

132
00:10:37,352 --> 00:10:42,900
She didn't. She surveyed the ground, found it
indefensible, and told me so to my face.

133
00:10:42,920 --> 00:10:48,532
That isn't capitulation. That's the first competent
thing any government has done about

134
00:10:48,552 --> 00:10:49,763
us in twenty years.

135
00:10:50,283 --> 00:10:51,629
You liked her.

136
00:10:52,149 --> 00:10:54,027
I respected her. It's rarer.

137
00:10:56,048 --> 00:11:01,844
Do you remember what you said to me? In the car, in
the rain, in the first week. You said:

138
00:11:01,864 --> 00:11:04,235
you lost because they saw you coming.

139
00:11:04,754 --> 00:11:05,610
I remember.

140
00:11:06,129 --> 00:11:11,909
They see you coming. That woman sees you coming.
The policeman saw you coming sixteen

141
00:11:11,929 --> 00:11:17,777
years ago, in four frames of a dashboard camera. The
Chinese woman has seen you coming

142
00:11:17,797 --> 00:11:21,939
since she listened to your silences.
They all see you coming.

143
00:11:23,339 --> 00:11:29,143
And they cannot do anything about it. Which means it
was never about being seen at all.

144
00:11:31,663 --> 00:11:37,593
The Wesker half of the mind sat with that for a
long time, over the dark Atlantic, at

145
00:11:37,613 --> 00:11:39,273
forty-one thousand feet.

146
00:11:39,842 --> 00:11:45,283
It was, he realised, the first thing the old voice
had ever taught him that he had not

147
00:11:45,303 --> 00:11:46,172
already known.

148
00:11:46,741 --> 00:11:52,038
It had never been about being unseen. Hiding had
only ever been a way of buying time.

149
00:11:52,058 --> 00:11:57,668
The thing that actually mattered — the thing he had
been building for twenty years without

150
00:11:57,688 --> 00:12:03,297
ever quite saying it to himself — was to become
necessary. And a necessary thing can stand

151
00:12:03,317 --> 00:12:08,802
in a cathedral square in broad daylight, in front of
four hundred cameras, and brush the

152
00:12:08,822 --> 00:12:12,618
dust off its shoulder, and ask whether
everyone is all right.

153
00:12:14,807 --> 00:12:15,407
Huh.

154
00:12:15,927 --> 00:12:16,793
Yes.

155
00:12:18,943 --> 00:12:23,723
Integration. Host coherence,
ninety-seven percent.

156
00:12:27,313 --> 00:12:28,194
Zero.

157
00:12:28,764 --> 00:12:34,484
He looked at that number for a long time, in the dark,
with the ocean going past underneath.

158
00:12:35,053 --> 00:12:40,299
In the first year there had been four hundred
arguments. Last year there had been two.

159
00:12:40,319 --> 00:12:45,564
This year there had been none at all — and he had
not noticed, which was the part that

160
00:12:45,584 --> 00:12:48,013
should have frightened him, and did not.
